[1m§2  erosion.rs — the MFD routing operator[0m
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Drainage solves  (I − Wᵀ)·A = q.  A zero eigenvalue of (I − Wᵀ) is an
   eigenvalue 1 of Wᵀ: a CLOSED CIRCULATION — cells handing flow round a loop,
   leaking none, so a nonzero drainage field exists with NO SOURCE. Water running
   in a circle on a hillside, forever. That is the router's rank deficiency, and
   it is the discrete ∇×∇φ ≠ 0 pathology.

   PIN — our W vs the kernel's own `drainage` field:
      Priority-Flood raised pits by up to 0.000 m (its ε-gradient; expected)
      max |ours − kernel| / max(drainage) = 1.268e-1
      [1;31m✗ TRANSCRIPTION NOT PINNED — the W below is not the kernel's.[0m
   (I − Wᵀ) on a 24×24 real land patch, 576 cells:
      σ_min = 3.1731e-2   σ_max = 2.2065e0   cond = 6.95e1
      zero singular values (tol = 1e-8·σ_max): 0    spectral gap: inf

   [1;32mCLEAN.[0m (I − Wᵀ) is nonsingular. There is NO closed circulation: MFD's
   flow graph is a strict DAG (weights only ever go to cells that are LOWER, so
   a cycle would need a cell below itself). [1mMFD cannot make water run in a circle.[0m
   Its sin is a BIAS in DIRECTION (grid_lab §9a, measured), not a null space.
      asymmetry ‖A−Aᵀ‖/‖A‖ = 0.733 (advective, as expected — singular values are the honest form)

