   [1mTHE SECOND MECHANISM — the Jarrett slope-roughness term is a POSITIVE FEEDBACK[0m
      water.rs computes roughness from the LOCAL WATER-SURFACE SLOPE, per pipe:
          n = min(n_base + 1.6·(head/l), 0.13)          [Jarrett 1984, linearised]
      and friction divides the flux by (1 + dt·g·n²·v / h^4ᐟ³). So:

        a cell that gains water  →  its surface steepens  →  n RISES  →  its outflow
        FALLS  →  it gains more water.  [1mThat loop closes with the wrong sign.[0m

      It should switch off once n saturates at the 0.13 cap (dn/dhead = 0 there).
      Prediction: unstable on GENTLE slopes (n uncapped), quiet in the middle band
